## Bash (Unix shell)

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash

# read_lines.sh -----------------------------------------------------------
read_file_line_by_line() {
    local path="$1"
    shift               # consume $path so we can use "$@" safely later

    while IFS= read -r line; do
        echo "$line"     # replace with whatever processing you need
    done < "$path"
}

# Example usage -----------------------------------------------------------
if [[ $# -eq 0 ]]; then
    echo "Usage: $0 <file>"
    exit 1
fi

read_file_line_by_line "$@"
```

*Why it works*: `while IFS= read -r line` reads the file descriptor of `$path` one line at a time. Bash’s built‑in loop avoids loading the whole file into memory.

