### Quick comparison table

| Language | How it streams | Memory footprint (typical) |
|----------|----------------|----------------------------|
| TypeScript (`readline`) | Node.js readable stream + async iterator | **≈ constant** – only one line in RAM at a time |
| Python `for line in f:` | Context‑manager iteration over file object | **≈ constant** – single buffered line (usually < 1 KB) |
| Go channel goroutine | Buffered scanner → channel → consumer loop | **≈ constant** – one line per buffer slot |
| Bash while/`read` | Shell builtin reads from FD of the opened file | **≈ constant** – each iteration holds a single string |

All four approaches are safe for arbitrarily large files because they never allocate memory proportional to the file size. Choose the language that best fits your project’s stack.

