## 1️⃣ Go  

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

// Counter is an integer protected by a mutex.
type Counter struct {
	value int
	mu    sync.Mutex // ← the mutex protecting the counter
}

// NewCounter returns a new, zeroed Counter.
func NewCounter() *Counter { return &Counter{} }

// Get returns the current value (read‑only).
func (c *Counter) Get() int {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	return c.value
}

// Inc increments the counter by n. Returns the new value atomically.
func (c *Counter) Inc(n int) int {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	c.value += n
	return c.value
}

/* -------------------------------------------------
   Example usage – concurrent reads & writes
--------------------------------------------------- */
func main() {
	ctr := NewCounter()

	var wg sync.WaitGroup

	wg.Add(5) // 5 goroutines that will increment many times
	for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
		go func(id int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			for j := 0; j < 10000; j++ {
				ctr.Inc(1)
			}
		}(id)
	}

	wg.Wait() // wait for all increments to finish

	fmt.Printf("Final value: %d\n", ctr.Get())
}
```

**Why it works**

* `sync.Mutex` guarantees that only one goroutine can execute the body of `Inc` (or `Get`) at a time.
* Lock/Unlock pairs are wrapped by helper methods (`Inc`, `Get`) to keep callers clean.

