## 1️⃣ Go – Using `sync/async` channels

```go
// pipeline.go ---------------------------------------------------------------

package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
   Stage A – Producer (writes data into a channel)
------------------------------------------------------------------- */
func stageA(out chan<- int, wg *sync.WaitGroup) {
	defer wg.Done()
	fmt.Println("🔹 Stage A: producing numbers 1‑5")
	for i := 1; i <= 5; i++ {
		out <- i          // push each number onto the channel
	}
	close(out)           // signal that no more items will arrive
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
   Stage B – Transformer (reads from a channel, does work, writes out)
------------------------------------------------------------------- */
func stageB(in <-chan int, out chan<- string, wg *sync.WaitGroup) {
	defer wg.Done()
	fmt.Println("🔹 Stage B: transforming numbers → words")
	for n := range in { // consume until the upstream closes
		out <- fmt.Sprintf("%d -> %s", n, wordFor(n))
	}
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
   Helper – map number → English word (just for demo)
------------------------------------------------------------------- */
func wordFor(num int) string {
	switch num {
	case 1: return "one"
	case 2: return "two"
	case 3: return "three"
	case 4: return "four"
	case 5: return "five"
	default: return ""
	}
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
   Stage C – Consumer (reads from a channel and does something with it)
------------------------------------------------------------------- */
func stageC(in <-chan string, wg *sync.WaitGroup) {
	defer wg.Done()
	fmt.Println("🔹 Stage C: consuming transformed strings")
	for msg := range in { // consume until the upstream closes
		fmt.Printf("* %s\n", msg)
	}
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
   Main – orchestrates the three stages with buffered channels.
------------------------------------------------------------------- */
func main() {
	var wg sync.WaitGroup

	// 1️⃣ Producer → Transformer (buffer size = number of items, here 5)
	aToB := make(chan int, 5)

	// 2️⃣ Transformer → Consumer
	bToC := make(chan string, 5)

	wg.Add(3) // three goroutines

	go stageA(aToB, &wg)
	go stageB(aToB, bToC, &wg)
	go stageC(bToC, &wg)

	wg.Wait() // wait for all stages to finish
}
```

### What the pipeline does  

| Step | Description |
|------|-------------|
| **Stage A** (`stageA`) | Generates a simple sequence `1‑5` and pushes each integer onto an **unbuffered channel**. The goroutine closes the channel once done, signalling that no more values will be sent. |
| **Stage B** (`stageB`) | Reads from the same channel (the buffer size matches the producer count). For every number it converts it to a word (“one”, “two”, …) and writes that string onto another buffered channel. The transformation is deliberately cheap – in real code you could run CPU‑heavy work here. |
| **Stage C** (`stageC`) | Consumes the transformed strings from the downstream buffer, printing each line prefixed with `*`. Because the consumer reads until the upstream closes, it never blocks waiting for more data. |

