## 3. Go

```go
package main

import (
	"errors"
	"sync"
)

// MessageQueue implements a thread‑safe FIFO queue.
type MessageQueue struct {
	queue []interface{} // items stored here
	mu    sync.Mutex    // protects the slice
}

// Enqueue adds an item to the back of the queue.
func (q *MessageQueue) Enqueue(item interface{}) {
	q.mu.Lock()
	defer q.mu.Unlock()
	q.queue = append(q.queue, item)
}

// Dequeue removes and returns the front item.
// Panics if called on an empty queue.
func (q *MessageQueue) Dequeue() interface{} {
	q.mu.Lock()
	defer q.mu.Unlock()

	if len(q.queue) == 0 {
		panic(errors.New("dequeue from empty MessageQueue"))
	}
	item := q.queue[0]
	q.queue = q.queue[1:] // drop first element
	return item
}

// Peek returns the front item without removing it.
// Returns nil if the queue is empty.
func (q *MessageQueue) Peek() interface{} {
	q.mu.Lock()
	defer q.mu.Unlock()

	if len(q.queue) == 0 {
		return nil
	}
	return q.queue[0]
}
```

### How it works
* **`sync.Mutex`** gives exclusive access to the underlying slice.  
  * The `Lock()/Unlock()` pattern (or the more concise `defer q.mu.Unlock()`) ensures that only one goroutine can mutate or read at a time.
* `append(q.queue, item)` creates a new underlying array if needed; this is cheap for small queues and works correctly even when concurrent calls are racing.

### Usage example

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"time"
)

func main() {
	q := &MessageQueue{}

	for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
		go func(i int) {
			time.Sleep(time.Second * time.Duration(i))
			q.Enqueue(i)
		}(i)
	}

	for !isEmpty(q) {
		fmt.Printf("dequeued: %v\n", q.Dequeue())
	}
}

func isEmpty(q *MessageQueue) bool {
	q.mu.Lock()
	defer q.mu.Unlock()
	return len(q.queue) == 0
}
