Below are three self‑contained examples that implement a **basic Observer (publish/subscribe) pattern** – objects can register listeners for named events and later emit those events so all interested parties get notified.

---

## 1️⃣ TypeScript  

```ts
// ---------------------------------------------------
// observer.ts – simple EventEmitter implementation
// ---------------------------------------------------
export interface EventListener<T> {
    (payload?: T): void;
}

/**
 * A minimal Pub/Sub system.
 *
 * - `on(name, listener)`   → subscribe a callback for an event name.
 * - `off(name, listener?)`→ unsubscribe.  If no listener is given,
 *                           all listeners for that name are removed.
 * - `emit(name, payload?)`→ notify every subscriber of the named event.
 */
export class EventEmitter {
    private handlers: Record<string, EventListener<any>[]> = {};

    /** Subscribe a callback to an event name (may be called multiple times). */
    on(name: string, listener: EventListener<any>): void {
        if (!this.handlers[name]) this.handlers[name] = [];
        this.handlers[name].push(listener);
    }

    /** Unsubscribe – removes only the supplied listener for that name. */
    off(name: string, listener?: EventListener<any>): void {
        const list = this.handlers[name];
        if (!list) return;
        if (listener === undefined) {
            // remove all listeners
            delete this.handlers[name];
        } else {
            this.handlers[name] = list.filter(l => l !== listener);
        }
    }

    /** Notify all subscribers of a given event name. */
    emit(name: string, payload?: any): void {
        const handlers = this.handlers[name]?.slice() ?? []; // make a copy
        for (const cb of handlers) {
            cb(payload);
        }
    }
}
```

### Usage example

```ts
// ---------------------------------------------------
// demo.ts – how to use the EventEmitter
// ---------------------------------------------------
import { EventEmitter } from './observer';

/* 1️⃣ Create an event bus */
const bus = new EventEmitter();

/* 2️⃣ Define some listeners */
bus.on('message', (msg: string) => {
    console.log(`[Listener A] Received message: ${msg}`);
});

bus.on('alert',   (payload: {level: 'high'|'low'; msg: string}) => {
    const lvl = payload.level;
    console.log(`[Listener B – ${lvl}] ${payload.msg}`);
});

/* 3️⃣ Emit events */
bus.emit('message',          'Hello, world!');
bus.emit('alert',           {level: 'high',   msg: 'Critical error'});
bus.emit('alert',           {level: 'low',    msg: 'Minor warning'});

/* 4️⃣ Unsubscribe (optional) */
// bus.off('message', listenerA); // removes only the first listener
```

**Key points**

* `EventEmitter` is a **class**, not a global singleton – you can have many independent buses.
* Listeners are stored in an array per event name, so multiple callbacks can be attached to the same topic.
* The generic type (`T`) lets TypeScript infer payload shape (e.g., `{level: 'high'; msg:string}`).

